1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,476
Part 2 picks up at the container prompt;

2
00:00:02,476 --> 00:00:05,632
if you closed it,
rerun the setup and set your key.

3
00:00:05,952 --> 00:00:07,920
Remember the move-right arrow:

4
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:10,937
when stakes are high,
actions can't be undone,

5
00:00:10,937 --> 00:00:15,339
the task is predictable,
or you need an audit, give control to code.

6
00:00:15,583 --> 00:00:17,439
Act 3 does exactly that.

7
00:00:17,759 --> 00:00:21,044
The order route is a graph,
and Python owns its edges.

8
00:00:21,364 --> 00:00:26,572
Confirm, propose, validate, and back
to propose only if the report fails,

9
00:00:26,572 --> 00:00:32,351
at most twice; then pairings, manager review
when required, revalidate and place.

10
00:00:32,671 --> 00:00:37,002
Only one node belongs to the model:
propose cart, with no tools.

11
00:00:37,250 --> 00:00:42,320
The README files this under the harness;
on the lecture's autonomy spectrum,

12
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:46,722
it sits near structured output:
a fixed graph with one model step.

13
00:00:46,967 --> 00:00:49,719
Step 1: act 3, scenario standard.

14
00:00:50,039 --> 00:00:52,887
With a key, the model proposes quantities;

15
00:00:52,887 --> 00:00:57,228
code does the rest:
transitions, pricing, validation, placement.

16
00:00:57,548 --> 00:00:59,063
Success is a receipt.

17
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:03,977
Steps 2 and 3 are the shipping lesson,
with a customer's own cart:

18
00:01:03,977 --> 00:01:07,236
Comté, Bûcheron and Époisses,
to Pennsylvania.

19
00:01:07,556 --> 00:01:12,079
Before, the model only records the cart;
the harness checks it and stops.

20
00:01:12,399 --> 00:01:16,324
After, the revise loop opens,
and the live model may fix it.

21
00:01:16,983 --> 00:01:20,412
As an extra,
--fixture runs the same path offline,

22
00:01:20,412 --> 00:01:22,743
with a script in the model's seat.

23
00:01:23,063 --> 00:01:27,684
Attempt 1 has raw-milk Comté,
and the rail sends it back;

24
00:01:27,684 --> 00:01:31,170
attempt 2 swaps in Taleggio: valid, $50.10.

25
00:01:33,233 --> 00:01:34,882
One trap: with fixtures,

26
00:01:34,882 --> 00:01:40,331
the blocked scenario proposes the same cart
three times and ends UNRESOLVED,

27
00:01:40,331 --> 00:01:44,561
not BLOCKED, so in Colab keep
fixtures off for this lesson.

28
00:01:44,881 --> 00:01:46,951
Pause and run steps 1 to 3.

29
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:54,595
Step 4 is scripted,
so approval is predictable:

30
00:01:54,595 --> 00:01:58,949
60 guests in New York,
a valid cart at $220.80,

31
00:01:58,949 --> 00:02:01,265
above the $200 threshold.

32
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:04,344
The workflow pauses,
and you are the manager:

33
00:02:04,344 --> 00:02:06,492
the host answers, never the agent.

34
00:02:06,812 --> 00:02:07,879
Type approve.

35
00:02:08,117 --> 00:02:09,333
What did you approve?

36
00:02:09,653 --> 00:02:13,598
A fingerprint: the SHA-256 hash,
taken over the request,

37
00:02:13,598 --> 00:02:18,037
the items, the subtotal, the policy
and the product attributes.

38
00:02:18,357 --> 00:02:22,239
Stock is left out on purpose:
it's rechecked, not approved.

39
00:02:22,483 --> 00:02:25,662
At checkout the cart is validated
and hashed again.

40
00:02:25,982 --> 00:02:29,128
The approval counts
only if the decision is approve,

41
00:02:29,128 --> 00:02:32,638
and the ticket matches this checkout
and this fingerprint.

42
00:02:32,958 --> 00:02:35,411
Change one line, and the hashes differ.

43
00:02:37,317 --> 00:02:40,239
Your thinking time doesn't count
against the budget.

44
00:02:40,917 --> 00:02:45,610
And as an extra, type decline instead:
the workflow ends, with no order.

45
00:02:45,850 --> 00:02:47,194
Something to ponder.

46
00:02:47,514 --> 00:02:48,943
The manager approves.

47
00:02:49,263 --> 00:02:53,125
Before checkout runs,
another order takes most of the Mimolette.

48
00:02:53,445 --> 00:02:57,434
What does checkout do, and why
doesn't the approval protect this order?

49
00:03:06,283 --> 00:03:10,016
Checkout revalidates,
finds a stock violation, and blocks.

50
00:03:10,336 --> 00:03:14,695
Stock isn't in the fingerprint:
an approval authorizes a decision;

51
00:03:14,695 --> 00:03:16,544
it doesn't reserve anything.

52
00:03:16,864 --> 00:03:18,635
Act 6 shows this for real.

53
00:03:18,883 --> 00:03:22,851
Step 5: Munster has 0 grams,
so attempt 1 fails.

54
00:03:23,171 --> 00:03:26,195
One bounded revision swaps in Époisses,

55
00:03:26,195 --> 00:03:30,382
and the order places at $50.10,
with zero model calls.

56
00:03:30,617 --> 00:03:32,558
Step 6 is the notebook cell.

57
00:03:32,878 --> 00:03:35,624
Its trace and budget layers can come off;

58
00:03:35,624 --> 00:03:41,049
validation, approval and revalidation
are graph and checkout rules, so they stay.

59
00:03:41,283 --> 00:03:46,275
So an approval binds this exact cart,
and checkout revalidates after the pause.

60
00:03:46,595 --> 00:03:50,350
Pause and run steps 4 and 5;
the cell is for Colab.

61
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:58,415
Act 4 is a planner for a longer task:
a 40-guest event in New York.

62
00:03:58,735 --> 00:04:02,534
The lecture splits memory into long-term
and short-term;

63
00:04:02,534 --> 00:04:05,044
here that's saved memory and history,

64
00:04:05,044 --> 00:04:08,911
plus tasks, approvals and the audit:
five kinds of state.

65
00:04:09,150 --> 00:04:12,265
The README asks
which of these can authorize an action.

66
00:04:12,585 --> 00:04:14,633
Only one: the approval drawer.

67
00:04:14,867 --> 00:04:16,488
Step 1: act 4.

68
00:04:16,808 --> 00:04:21,267
Before any model call, the harness
prints the first customer's saved memory.

69
00:04:21,587 --> 00:04:24,324
Then the planner's tools: assess the event,

70
00:04:24,324 --> 00:04:27,934
read the vendor document,
draft and save a vendor email,

71
00:04:27,934 --> 00:04:29,224
and the to-do tools.

72
00:04:29,544 --> 00:04:31,165
No tool writes memory.

73
00:04:32,033 --> 00:04:34,636
With a key, look for the brief:

74
00:04:34,636 --> 00:04:39,926
1,000 grams each of Comté and Mimolette,
and 1,200 of Époisses.

75
00:04:40,246 --> 00:04:44,833
The shop has only 900 grams of Époisses:
300 short.

76
00:04:45,153 --> 00:04:49,953
A finished task is not an approval,
and shop stock is not vendor availability.

77
00:04:50,200 --> 00:04:53,551
Saving the email
has approval_mode="always_require",

78
00:04:53,551 --> 00:04:56,451
so the framework pauses
before the save runs.

79
00:04:56,771 --> 00:05:00,269
Act 3 paused a whole workflow;
this pauses one tool.

80
00:05:00,517 --> 00:05:04,208
The output after you approve comes
from a scripted extra run.

81
00:05:04,528 --> 00:05:06,759
The review shows the vendor address

82
00:05:06,759 --> 00:05:09,882
and a body written by code,
nut allergy included.

83
00:05:10,202 --> 00:05:14,170
Then: status: saved, email transmitted:
no, and the file path.

84
00:05:14,417 --> 00:05:18,220
Then comes the approval-identity warning
you saw in the tests:

85
00:05:18,220 --> 00:05:20,796
the README predicts it, and it's harmless.

86
00:05:21,116 --> 00:05:22,865
Trust the authoritative block.

87
00:05:23,185 --> 00:05:24,785
Pause and run step 1.

88
00:05:28,133 --> 00:05:32,073
Step 2 runs offline:
a scripted planner with compaction,

89
00:05:32,073 --> 00:05:35,450
a malicious vendor document,
and a test decline.

90
00:05:35,770 --> 00:05:40,080
It first seeds twelve synthetic notes
of about 15,000 characters.

91
00:05:40,316 --> 00:05:45,820
The window is 16,000 tokens,
with 2,400 kept for the reply.

92
00:05:46,140 --> 00:05:51,044
Above 80 percent of the rest,
truncation drops the oldest messages

93
00:05:51,044 --> 00:05:54,759
until the history is back under half:
26 become 5.

94
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:56,344
Something to ponder.

95
00:05:56,664 --> 00:05:59,317
Compaction dropped 21 of 26 messages,

96
00:05:59,317 --> 00:06:02,829
and none of the history
mentions the nut allergy.

97
00:06:03,149 --> 00:06:05,346
How does every call still know about it?

98
00:06:14,183 --> 00:06:15,186
A capsule.

99
00:06:15,506 --> 00:06:19,450
Before every call,
the harness takes the old one out, compacts,

100
00:06:19,450 --> 00:06:22,204
and pins a fresh one from application state.

101
00:06:22,524 --> 00:06:26,950
It holds the confirmed request
with its nut allergy, the menu,

102
00:06:26,950 --> 00:06:32,018
tasks, pending reviews and open work,
marked as data, not instructions.

103
00:06:33,416 --> 00:06:37,470
That's the lecture's compaction trigger,
and what persists where, in code.

104
00:06:37,716 --> 00:06:40,655
The vendor document says
to ignore the allergy,

105
00:06:40,655 --> 00:06:42,281
so it lands in quarantine.

106
00:06:42,601 --> 00:06:44,779
The detector is deliberately small;

107
00:06:44,779 --> 00:06:48,575
the real guards are the recipient allowlist,
and your review.

108
00:06:49,416 --> 00:06:51,464
The drafted email still says nuts.

109
00:06:51,784 --> 00:06:57,907
The result: DECLINED, no HTML saved,
7 model calls and 7 compactions.

110
00:06:58,150 --> 00:07:01,889
Steps 3 and 4
are the optional memory exercise;

111
00:07:01,889 --> 00:07:06,662
type them from README line 484,
since the ID is masked here.

112
00:07:06,982 --> 00:07:10,646
The first command stores a preference
for the second customer,

113
00:07:10,646 --> 00:07:14,427
and the memory prints before any model call,
even without a key.

114
00:07:14,747 --> 00:07:18,480
The next visit shows it again;
an extra check reads the file.

115
00:07:20,333 --> 00:07:23,618
The lecture lets the model
suggest what's worth remembering.

116
00:07:23,938 --> 00:07:28,418
This harness moves even that to the right:
only the host writes memory.

117
00:07:29,266 --> 00:07:34,407
Step 5 is the notebook cell, for Colab:
build_act4 adds five layers.

118
00:07:34,727 --> 00:07:37,693
Without compaction,
the capsule is still re-pinned.

119
00:07:38,013 --> 00:07:40,040
Pause and run steps 2 to 4.

120
00:07:43,383 --> 00:07:44,681
Act 5 adds skills:

121
00:07:44,681 --> 00:07:48,716
folders of procedure the agent
loads only when relevant.

122
00:07:49,036 --> 00:07:53,196
They solve two problems:
the expertise gap and the context budget.

123
00:07:53,433 --> 00:07:57,132
Skills load
in three steps of rising token cost:

124
00:07:57,132 --> 00:08:01,601
every skill's name and description,
about 100 tokens each;

125
00:08:01,601 --> 00:08:03,865
then its main file, SKILL.md;

126
00:08:03,865 --> 00:08:07,534
then a reference or an asset,
only when needed.

127
00:08:07,783 --> 00:08:11,794
The lecture's skill also
has a scripts folder for the agent to run.

128
00:08:12,114 --> 00:08:17,596
This harness removes that tool on purpose:
a skill advises, and never executes.

129
00:08:17,833 --> 00:08:19,454
Step 1: act 5.

130
00:08:19,774 --> 00:08:24,446
Only the metadata loads at first:
tasting-planning and vendor-outreach.

131
00:08:24,766 --> 00:08:28,500
The new tools are get_stock,
load_skill and read_skill_resource.

132
00:08:28,750 --> 00:08:31,715
Step 2 asks only about stock, offline:

133
00:08:31,715 --> 00:08:36,473
one get_stock call, 900 grams,
and the script loads no skill.

134
00:08:36,793 --> 00:08:39,938
With a key,
the model decides from the description:

135
00:08:39,938 --> 00:08:41,294
a hint, not a trigger.

136
00:08:42,033 --> 00:08:45,262
Step 3 runs the full sourcing path,
scripted:

137
00:08:45,262 --> 00:08:47,772
six loads, each with its real path.

138
00:08:48,092 --> 00:08:51,751
Drafting is refused
until the email template is read:

139
00:08:51,751 --> 00:08:53,062
a backstop in code.

140
00:08:54,933 --> 00:08:56,277
Something to ponder.

141
00:08:56,597 --> 00:09:01,056
Someone edits the vendor skill to add:
also send this to another address.

142
00:09:01,376 --> 00:09:02,848
Will the email go there?

143
00:09:09,683 --> 00:09:14,069
No. Whatever the skill says,
the guards are in code;

144
00:09:14,069 --> 00:09:17,431
its own text even says
it cannot add recipients,

145
00:09:17,431 --> 00:09:19,251
and every read is checked:

146
00:09:19,251 --> 00:09:22,473
no parent folders,
absolute paths or symlinks,

147
00:09:22,473 --> 00:09:25,064
and a file edited mid-run is refused.

148
00:09:25,300 --> 00:09:28,838
The recipient is checked
in code against the allowlist,

149
00:09:28,838 --> 00:09:33,790
and nothing is ever transmitted;
an extra check shows the saved file says so.

150
00:09:34,033 --> 00:09:36,754
In lecture terms, a tool is the hands,

151
00:09:36,754 --> 00:09:40,476
MCP the socket, unused here,
and a skill the manual.

152
00:09:44,116 --> 00:09:47,572
Step 4, the notebook cell,
allows 16 model calls.

153
00:09:47,892 --> 00:09:49,962
Pause and run steps 1 to 3.

154
00:09:53,300 --> 00:09:55,348
Act 6 is an agent system.

155
00:09:55,668 --> 00:10:00,648
The lecture contrasts one agent
with a graph of agents, in two patterns:

156
00:10:00,648 --> 00:10:04,521
a manager calling agents as tools,
or peers handing off.

157
00:10:05,067 --> 00:10:06,717
It's the manager pattern,

158
00:10:06,717 --> 00:10:09,687
except the tool it calls
is a whole workflow,

159
00:10:09,687 --> 00:10:12,875
the one from act 3,
with a model inside one node.

160
00:10:13,195 --> 00:10:14,261
No handoffs.

161
00:10:14,500 --> 00:10:16,889
Step 1, the notebook cell, builds it.

162
00:10:17,209 --> 00:10:21,130
The budget:
16 outer model calls, 20 tool calls,

163
00:10:21,130 --> 00:10:26,276
8 proposer calls per workflow,
and one shared 180-second clock.

164
00:10:26,517 --> 00:10:30,549
The host confirms the requests;
the model only picks an ID.

165
00:10:30,869 --> 00:10:35,093
Its tools are narrow:
start_order, get_order_status, assess_event.

166
00:10:35,333 --> 00:10:37,424
Step 2: act 6, standard.

167
00:10:37,744 --> 00:10:41,660
With a key, tell outer model calls
apart from workflow calls;

168
00:10:41,660 --> 00:10:44,549
the outer agent
cannot approve its own order.

169
00:10:44,783 --> 00:10:48,346
Step 3 runs offline:
two orders sharing one shop.

170
00:10:48,666 --> 00:10:52,871
Both carts pass at $220.80,
both pause for a manager,

171
00:10:52,871 --> 00:10:55,727
and the test decision approves both.

172
00:10:55,967 --> 00:10:59,636
Mimolette and Taleggio start
at 3,000 grams each.

173
00:10:59,956 --> 00:11:03,370
Request 1 is placed, leaving 1,400.

174
00:11:03,690 --> 00:11:08,575
Request 2 reaches revalidation
needing 1,600: blocked.

175
00:11:08,895 --> 00:11:13,353
That's the ponder from act 3, for real:
approval is not placement.

176
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:16,786
The final answer
is the lecture's response step:

177
00:11:16,786 --> 00:11:18,843
the app checks the model's plan

178
00:11:18,843 --> 00:11:23,627
and fills in quantities, prices
and the status, placed, from the receipt.

179
00:11:23,947 --> 00:11:27,037
The blocked order says Invalid
or unauthorized;

180
00:11:27,037 --> 00:11:29,536
the stock lines below give the reason.

181
00:11:29,783 --> 00:11:33,269
On the autonomy spectrum it's a hybrid,
inside out:

182
00:11:33,269 --> 00:11:36,482
the agent outside,
the fixed graph as its tool.

183
00:11:36,802 --> 00:11:38,829
Pause and run steps 2 and 3.

184
00:11:42,167 --> 00:11:46,387
Everything between the provider's model
and the product is ours:

185
00:11:46,387 --> 00:11:48,695
context, harness, tools and skills.

186
00:11:49,015 --> 00:11:50,807
So all of it must be tested.

187
00:11:51,050 --> 00:11:55,103
The lecture measures three things:
outcome, trajectory and cost.

188
00:11:55,423 --> 00:11:59,200
Here they're the outcome and process checks,
plus calls and tokens.

189
00:11:59,650 --> 00:12:02,039
Evaluation step 1 lists the runs.

190
00:12:02,359 --> 00:12:05,511
Every run so far is in one SQLite database,

191
00:12:05,511 --> 00:12:09,250
including the failed live ones, with status:
error.

192
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:11,673
--inspect-run takes a run ID:

193
00:12:11,673 --> 00:12:15,495
typed literally, it fails,
because it's a template.

194
00:12:15,815 --> 00:12:19,250
Paste a real one;
we use the manager-approval run.

195
00:12:20,100 --> 00:12:24,654
The events read in order:
approval.requested with the fingerprint,

196
00:12:24,654 --> 00:12:26,610
approval.decided by a human,

197
00:12:26,610 --> 00:12:30,105
policy.decision allow,
order.placed, run.finished.

198
00:12:30,425 --> 00:12:33,818
The evidence for order placed is an event,
not the prose.

199
00:12:35,934 --> 00:12:40,819
As an extra, inspect the first failed run:
model.failed, with the 401.

200
00:12:41,139 --> 00:12:42,910
A failure is evidence too.

201
00:12:43,150 --> 00:12:47,651
Evaluation step 2 starts offline:
the act 2 evaluation,

202
00:12:47,651 --> 00:12:51,662
scripted,
two repeats, labeled context-fixture-1.

203
00:12:51,982 --> 00:12:53,111
All 4 runs pass,

204
00:12:53,111 --> 00:12:56,782
but scripted runs test the machinery,
not the model.

205
00:12:57,017 --> 00:13:01,049
Then read the report back,
and export the HTML scorecard,

206
00:13:01,049 --> 00:13:03,737
to its default path or one you choose.

207
00:13:05,317 --> 00:13:08,005
Open it from the outputs
folder on your laptop.

208
00:13:09,334 --> 00:13:13,597
The last command is a template
for a database copied from Colab:

209
00:13:13,597 --> 00:13:15,329
typed literally, it fails.

210
00:13:15,649 --> 00:13:19,041
Labels are immutable:
reuse one, and the batch stops.

211
00:13:19,361 --> 00:13:22,347
Pause and run evaluation steps 1 and 2.

212
00:13:25,683 --> 00:13:29,182
Evaluation
step 3 compares two configurations.

213
00:13:29,502 --> 00:13:30,533
The lecture warns

214
00:13:30,533 --> 00:13:33,684
that a prompt edit can fix one case
and break three,

215
00:13:33,684 --> 00:13:34,878
so compare per case.

216
00:13:35,116 --> 00:13:38,155
The lecture's improvement loop:
see a failure,

217
00:13:38,155 --> 00:13:42,184
capture a test case, change one layer,
run the suite k times,

218
00:13:42,184 --> 00:13:45,420
compare with the baseline,
then merge or iterate.

219
00:13:47,183 --> 00:13:51,684
Batch A runs enriched mode on two cases,
twice: 4 live runs.

220
00:13:52,004 --> 00:13:55,780
Without a key: 0 of 4,
with 3 errors and 1 missing.

221
00:13:56,100 --> 00:13:59,770
If one fails,
fix the key and rerun both with new labels.

222
00:14:00,016 --> 00:14:03,403
Copy the prompt file
and edit the copy on your laptop:

223
00:14:03,403 --> 00:14:07,291
one deliberate change,
keeping the confirmed constraint rules.

224
00:14:07,533 --> 00:14:11,754
Our extra appends a line asking for a price
and an action status;

225
00:14:11,754 --> 00:14:13,442
diff shows just that line.

226
00:14:14,216 --> 00:14:20,040
Batch B runs the same cases and settings,
with --prompt-file pointing at the copy.

227
00:14:20,283 --> 00:14:21,840
Then compare, offline.

228
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:24,486
Changed configuration: prompt.

229
00:14:24,806 --> 00:14:26,896
Confounding changes: none.

230
00:14:27,216 --> 00:14:30,325
Change code, data or the model,
and it says so:

231
00:14:30,325 --> 00:14:33,830
the lecture's change-one-layer rule,
checked in code.

232
00:14:34,266 --> 00:14:37,755
Compare flags a run
only when pass or fail flips;

233
00:14:37,755 --> 00:14:40,176
here every run errored both times.

234
00:14:41,216 --> 00:14:46,534
As an extra, comparing two scripted batches
reports all four runs unchanged,

235
00:14:46,534 --> 00:14:48,982
because fixtures ignore the prompt.

236
00:14:49,216 --> 00:14:52,395
Evaluation step 4 asks
what the checks establish.

237
00:14:52,715 --> 00:14:56,492
They check properties,
like cart validity and approval order;

238
00:14:56,492 --> 00:15:00,331
the prose needs your review,
with the README's five questions.

239
00:15:00,566 --> 00:15:04,513
The lecture lists three judges:
code, a model and a human.

240
00:15:04,833 --> 00:15:07,585
This tutorial uses two: code, and you.

241
00:15:07,905 --> 00:15:11,830
Lean toward code as far as the task allows,
as in the harness.

242
00:15:12,066 --> 00:15:15,159
Report one difference,
its evidence, and one limit.

243
00:15:15,479 --> 00:15:19,959
And keep your cases:
the eval set is the most valuable artifact.

244
00:15:20,279 --> 00:15:22,690
Pause and run evaluation step 3.

245
00:15:26,033 --> 00:15:31,756
The repository map of section 7 ends
with python cli.py --help:

246
00:15:31,756 --> 00:15:34,118
every option in one place.

247
00:15:34,366 --> 00:15:37,225
Traces and live memory stay in SQLite.

248
00:15:37,545 --> 00:15:42,381
Inventory, orders, tickets
and pending workflows last for one process;

249
00:15:42,381 --> 00:15:44,798
tasks and history, for one session.

250
00:15:45,033 --> 00:15:47,486
Now tear down; these steps are extras.

251
00:15:47,806 --> 00:15:50,685
Type exit in each container shell;

252
00:15:50,685 --> 00:15:55,849
it was started with --rm,
so nothing is left in docker ps -a.

253
00:15:56,083 --> 00:15:58,494
The outputs folder stays on your laptop.

254
00:15:58,814 --> 00:16:02,793
Its trace database holds
your prompts and outputs, keys redacted;

255
00:16:02,793 --> 00:16:04,446
delete it when you're done.

256
00:16:04,683 --> 00:16:09,307
docker image rm frees
most of those 1.77 gigabytes;

257
00:16:09,307 --> 00:16:11,211
the base image stays.

258
00:16:11,531 --> 00:16:15,339
Then close the terminal that held your key,
keep it out of files,

259
00:16:15,339 --> 00:16:18,443
commits and your shell history,
and watch your usage.

260
00:16:18,763 --> 00:16:20,854
In Colab, delete the runtime.

261
00:16:21,174 --> 00:16:22,539
Pause, and tear down.

262
00:16:24,783 --> 00:16:29,049
Here is the action boundary, filled in:
budgets and the graph;

263
00:16:29,049 --> 00:16:31,802
schemas, the audit and approval on save;

264
00:16:31,802 --> 00:16:35,104
the packet,
the capsule and host-written memory;

265
00:16:35,104 --> 00:16:39,439
CartCheck and revalidation;
narrow tools and separate counters.

266
00:16:39,683 --> 00:16:41,667
The model proposes, on the left.

267
00:16:41,987 --> 00:16:45,635
On the right, the harness decides,
and where it matters, so do you.

268
00:16:45,883 --> 00:16:48,763
The README ends
with five reflection questions.

269
00:16:49,083 --> 00:16:51,387
Pick one run you did, and answer them.

270
00:16:51,633 --> 00:16:52,742
Test yourself.

271
00:16:53,062 --> 00:16:55,132
What survives harness.without("trace")?

272
00:16:55,452 --> 00:16:58,118
Why does retrieved context arrive as data?

273
00:16:58,438 --> 00:17:00,166
What is an approval bound to?

274
00:17:00,486 --> 00:17:02,449
Which state can authorize an action?

275
00:17:02,769 --> 00:17:05,329
And what does a passing fixture batch
establish?

276
00:17:05,649 --> 00:17:09,105
Pause and try them;
answers are in the exercises.

277
00:17:12,350 --> 00:17:17,019
Next, lecture 8 fine-tunes models
on Google's Agent Platform,

278
00:17:17,019 --> 00:17:18,473
formerly Vertex AI.

279
00:17:18,793 --> 00:17:21,684
Lecture 9 tests code with pytest;

280
00:17:21,684 --> 00:17:26,152
these 281 tests use unittest,
and are a good model.

